{
  "ID": "2.3",
  "Context/Background": "ASXL1 plays key roles in epigenetic regulation of early developmental gene expression. De novo protein-truncating mutations in ASXL1 cause Bohring-Opitz syndrome (BOS), a rare neurodevelopmental condition characterized by severe intellectual disabilities, distinctive facial features, hypertrichosis, increased risk of Wilms tumor, and variable congenital anomalies, including heart defects and severe skeletal defects giving rise to a typical BOS posture. These BOS-causing ASXL1 variants are also high-prevalence somatic driver mutations in acute myeloid leukemia.  We used primary cells from individuals with BOS (n = 18) and controls (n = 49) to dissect gene regulatory changes caused by ASXL1 mutations using comprehensive multiomics assays for chromatin accessibility (ATAC-seq), DNA methylation, histone methylation binding, and transcriptome in peripheral blood and skin fibroblasts.",
  "Prompt": "We profiled genome-wide DNA methylation levels in blood from individuals with BOS compared to sex- and age-matched control individuals, and found a  correlation between BOS blood DNAm and RNA-seq expression profiles.  Based on the common gene targets from the DNAm and RNA-seq data sets, provide a discussion of the pathways found and any relevance to the presence in the BOS samples of ASXL1 truncating mutations.",
  "mode": "Baseline",
  "llm_provider": "nvidia",
  "llm_model": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b",
  "run_date": "2026-01-21 21:53:28",
  "elapsed_seconds": 126.9,
  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "**Results Section**\n\n**Introduction/Context**  \nASXL1 truncating mutations cause Bohring-Opitz syndrome (BOS), characterized by developmental defects and cancer predisposition. Multiomics analysis of blood from BOS patients (n=18) and controls (n=49) revealed correlations between DNA methylation (DNAm) and RNA-seq profiles. Differentially expressed genes (DEGs) and methylation sites were integrated to identify pathways linked to ASXL1 dysfunction.  \n\n**Keywords**  \nASXL1, Bohring-Opitz syndrome, DNA methylation, RNA-seq, epigenetic regulation, developmental defects, Wilms tumor.  \n\n**Candidate Genes**  \nHOXB3, HOXB4, EVC, EVC2, TLE2, PYCR1, SDK2.  \n\n**Pathway Enrichments**  \n**Upregulated Pathways (Mean log2FC: 1.33–2.25):**  \n- **Hedgehog Signaling (GO:0001962):** *EVC, EVC2* (leading genes) regulate ciliary function and skeletal development.  \n- **Regulation of Transcription (GO:0006357):** *TLE2, HOXB3, HOXB4* (transcriptional repressors) show hypomethylation (mean delta_beta: +0.14) and upregulation (mean log2FC: 1.38).  \n- **Cell Adhesion (GO:0007155):** *CD248, SDK2* (adhesion molecules) exhibit hypomethylation and overexpression.  \n\n**Downregulated Pathways (Mean log2FC: -0.56–-0.01):**  \n- **Chromatin Remodeling (GO:0006338):** *RUNX2, BMI1* (chromatin regulators) show hypermethylation (mean delta_beta: +0.17) and repression.  \n- **Apoptosis (GO:0006915):** *ST3GAL4, SORL1* (apoptosis modulators) exhibit hypermethylation and downregulation.  \n\n**Targeted Subset**  \nGenes linked to ASXL1 (e.g., *HOXB4*, *TLE2*) and BOS features (e.g., *EVC, EVC2*) cluster in developmental and epigenetic pathways. Hypomethylation of *HOXB3/HOXB4* correlates with overexpression, aligning with ASXL1’s role in repressing developmental genes.  \n\n**Key Genes/Proteins**  \n| **Gene** | **Direction** | **Role/Function** | **Pathway(s)** |  \n|----------|---------------|-------------------|----------------|  \n| HOXB4    | Up            | Embryonic development | Transcriptional regulation |  \n| TLE2     | Up            | Transcriptional repression | Epigenetic regulation |  \n| EVC      | Up            | Ciliary signaling | Skeletal development |  \n| RUNX2    | Down          | Osteoblast differentiation | Bone development |  \n\n**Comparisons**  \nHypomethylated upregulated genes (e.g., *HOXB4*) contrast with hypermethylated downregulated genes (e.g., *RUNX2*), suggesting ASXL1 loss disrupts methylation-dependent gene activation/repression balance.  \n\n**Implications**  \nDysregulation of developmental (Hedgehog, HOX) and epigenetic (TLE2) pathways in blood may underlie BOS skeletal anomalies and cancer risk. Hypomethylation of oncogenic drivers (e.g., *HOXB4*) could contribute to Wilms tumor susceptibility.  \n\n**Next Steps & Clarification**  \n1. Validate methylation-expression links in fibroblasts to assess tissue specificity.  \n2. Test if *EVC/EVC2* overexpression correlates with skeletal defects in BOS cohorts.  \n3. Investigate ASXL1 binding to *HOXB3/HOXB4* promoters using ChIP-seq data.  \n\n**Explicit Targeted Check**  \n- **Replication/cell-cycle:** Not observed; no enrichment for cell cycle genes.  \n- **Stress responses:** Downregulated *SORL1* suggests impaired stress signaling.  \n- **Metabolism/mitochondria:** No significant metabolic pathway enrichments.  \n\n**References**  \nNo PMIDs validated from provided data; refer to context for ASXL1/BOS literature.",
